from banking to beer to Bermuda  Christianity to climate change and China  there are now more appgs than there are  sitting MPS with  746 active groups in the latest register  that number has almost doubled since  2015.  appg stands for all party parliamentary  group and they do what they say on the  tin they're formed by MPS from different  parties who come together to discuss an  issue a country or a sector they often  produce reports or hold events with  experts to discuss their subject they  have to be chaired by an MP and have  another five MPS stand as officers but  they can also have external members from  outside Parliament they're also informal  so unlike a select committee they have  no official status but they can use the  parliament logo on their Publications  and hold meetings in Parliament  crucially they don't receive any funding  from Parliament so rely on external  organizations to provide cash and other  benefits for them to operate  [Music]  funding can come from Charities to big  businesses to academic institutions but  four out of the top five biggest funders  of appgs since the last election are  lobbying groups this means they've  signed a register to declare that they  Lobby ministers on policy and  legislation on behalf of paying clients  appgs do valuable campaigning work to  ensure that neglected issues come to the  attention of politicians but to report  by Parliament standards committee last  year warned that they could represent  the next great parliamentary Scandal  while they said that lobbying is an  important part of a healthy democracy  and trade groups companies and  campaigners need to be heard they also  warned that there are few if any  safeguards in place in September the  government responded saying it agreed  that appgs were potentially vulnerable  to improper access and influence
 

